{
  "dom_id": "step-q103380",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-7≡27\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $-5≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $11≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $5∤(-7-27).$ Notice that $-7-27=-34,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $6∤(-5-15).$ Notice that $-5-15=-20,$ which is not divisible by $6.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $7\\,|\\,(11-18).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-18)÷7=-7÷7=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
